{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question","id":1150615,"title":"Reconciling the classical/technology-as-magic ideas (an outsiders question)?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-10-18T05:48:07-07:00","id":1150615,"title":"Reconciling the classical/technology-as-magic ideas (an outsiders question)?","content":"Discalimer: I have never played a Mage game in my life.<br /> <br /> With that out of the way, how does the setting, and more importantly, the players, reconcile the occultist/esotericist/spiritual ideas of the game (concepts which are, in our own reality, generally hostile to modernity and sometimes even hostile to the material world as such, late 1800's ideas about magnetism and mesmeism and such nothwithstanding) with the high-tech, Asimov-quote-inducing concepts like the Void Engineers.<br /> <br /> I can get behind the &quot;classical&quot; esotericist ideas, and I can get behind the &quot;Nazi UFOs powered by magic&quot; flair of the Technocracy, but not both. I get that both being equally true at the same time is a central conflict of the setting, but it just never really clicked for me.<br /> <br /> How do yyou do it, forum?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T06:38:28-07:00","id":1150622,"title":"","content":"The thing in Mage is... everything can be true, you're the one who forms your own reality. Exept there are billions of people on the planet and everyone has some kind of idea how reality should be like. Now you got a really big mess and that's where the consensus comes in. It takes all the &quot;agreed on&quot; aspects of peoples imaginations and puts them together in a more or less singular reality (not considering regional reality zones and stuff). So here you have quite the stable, fleshed out, capital-R Reality. This Reality is true to everyone.<br /> <br /> Exept there are mages, who basically say: &quot;Peoples reality is not my reality.&quot; And they influence Reality in a way they themselfes belief it should be instead. They can believe in everything they want, but it has to be a very fleshed out, powerfull belief. They have to reassure themselfes constantly that THIS is how it really should be. So your occultist summoning demons and the Void Engineer busting &quot;Extradimansional Entities&quot; are actually dealing with the same creatures, who use the same rules, but ones alien from another dimension is the other ones demon.<br /> <br /> There is one very major point about the Technocracy though. They don't believe in Magic (capital M). They believe in Science! There's no thing such as a demon summoning or homeopathic medicine or whatever. That's bullshit from a technocrats point of view. They work actively to make people believe science was real and magic was not, flipping the consensus in their favour.<br /> At the same time, mages have, by now, mostly adapted to the technologic world of the 21st century. Hermetics use smartphones and even most Verbena will probably have a Whatsapp group for their witches circle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13221-thevarulfen","name":"TheVarulfen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T08:48:25-07:00","id":1150658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheVarulfen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1150622#post1150622\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There is one very major point about the Technocracy though. They don't believe in Magic (capital M). They believe in Science! There's no thing such as a demon summoning or homeopathic medicine or whatever. That's bullshit from a technocrats point of view. They work actively to make people believe science was real and magic was not, flipping the consensus in their favour.<br /> At the same time, mages have, by now, mostly adapted to the technologic world of the 21st century. Hermetics use smartphones and even most Verbena will probably have a Whatsapp group for their witches circle.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that is one of the primary flaws of the setting and a major place in which the game loses people, now that it's not &quot;just Traditions&quot; and other Sects and Fashions are playable. Because it demands that the Technocracy to be inherently stupid to the point of non-functionality. Technocracts are GOING to encounter evidence of the actual, verifiable reality of the supernatural in one way or another unless they are VERY sheltered. So they have to be in a state of willfull ignorance or be so deeply programmed that they wouldn't really be functional as human beings much less as reality altering mages. Hell, they couldn't flip the Consensus in their favor, because they would be blind to anything that lay outside their field of vision. What would they be flipping?<br /> <br /> It really makes a Union game or a game containing Union personnel in a mixed setting nearly impossible. The role-playing is just so awkward and stilted as the Technocrats are continually loosing their shit and/or doing mental backflips to try and explain away what they are seeing.<br /> <br /> Now if the Union KNOWS there is such a thing as &quot;magic&quot;, then they can try to flip the Consensus away from that. That requires making the Union a little more intelligent and another, more rational reasoning (Rational? For the group that's supposed to be all about that? Why, I never!) Typically we've gone with &quot;Yes, we know we alter reality and we know they alter reality, but we are doing it the right, safe and sensible way, e.g. technology/science, and they are wildly irresponsible and using unsafe methodologies that have inherent problelmatic issues that are dangerous to not only themselves but to the Consensus.&quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T09:32:36-07:00","id":1150668,"title":"","content":"Rank and file Technocrats believe in the supernatural, they just think of it as corruption of the &quot;true&quot; reality. Higher-ups have the bigger picture but still favor something relatively close to Consensus reality. How does that make Technocrats stupid?<br /> <br /> Edit: changed a word.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 10-18-2017, 12:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T09:56:55-07:00","id":1150673,"title":"","content":"So why have the Technocrats as mages at all? Why can't they just be lower-case technocrat scientists/engineers who are decades ahead of the common usage of technology, and who wish to eradicate the wrong that is magic(k)?<br /> <br /> Sort of like how Arcanum: Of Steamworks and Magic Obscura pitted technology and magic against each other.<br /> <br /> Don't misunderstand me, I'm not trying to crap on the setting, I just don't see it as self-consistent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5238,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","user":"Herbert_West","date":". 10-18-2017, 01:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T11:01:34-07:00","id":1150700,"title":"","content":"Well, part of the idea of the Technocracy is that they are a foil for the Traditions. Technocrats are pursuing science that is so advanced and outside the norm that it is to normal people, the Sleepers, essentially magic. The Technocracy doesn't want to admit the supernatural exists, but their inspired inventions that exceed the bounds of current belief take time for people to accept. Think of people who are laggards on cell phones or won't use a computer and you see some of the trouble the Technocracy has in pushing science. For the Technocracy, properly controlled science leads to a better world for people. The Technocracy wants people to believe in the scientific method and refuses to acknowledge that each scientist makes their own reality with their hypothesis, or tries to underplay this.<br /> <br /> The Traditions, representing those who are supernaturally-oriented or use weird science (Sons of Ether and Virtual Adepts), know reality is shaped by will and as such, oppose the Technocracy trying to say 'this is reality.' Remember that Technocratic reality is very materialistic and excludes spiritual ideas and motivations--which is bad for most of the Traditions.<br /> <br /> If we go with a very simplified version of the Triad of Wyrm, Weaver and Wyld from the Werewolf game, I put the major mage factions as this:<br /> <br /> Nephandi--purely destructive and dangerous Wyrm. They seek to bring more death and disorder and destroy the world. Their foil in the Traditions is the Euthanatos, who clear the cancerous growths out of society by providing the Good Death.<br /> <br /> Traditions--A balance between the Triad, as they develop on individual terms, build coalitions and destroy ossified and problematic ideas. Each Tradition has its own focus, but combined they are working to create a better new reality on the ashes of old realities. The High Mythic Ages, when dragons lived and you had a medieval focus in Europe (basically the Ars Magica RPG) failed, but the golden age will return. The Sons of Ether and Virtual Adepts are the outliers, given they use technology as magick, but these two groups see individualism in their technology and reject the consensual, controlled reality of the Technocracy. For the Wyrm part, they are the good Wyrm for breaking down the Technocratic reality.<br /> <br /> Technocracy--The agents of the Weaver gone mad, the Technocracy wants control. If they could build a closed system where science explained everything, they would. So, the Technocracy hunts those who disagree and works to rob Sleepers of their initiative so there is no dissent. There are many shades of 1984, Brave New World and cyberpunk here, but over the years the Technocrats have gone from the powerful overarching boogeyman with Men in Black and other things out of conspiracy theory to a more nuanced faction. Still, they are scientists with a timetable and no brook no dissent, so like science in Soviet Russia, those who disagree with the science will be eliminated or reprogrammed to think as the Technocracy wants them to.<br /> <br /> Marauders--These mages are pure Wyld, with their own reality become everyone's reality around them when they can. Essentially Marauders represent mages withdrawing into schizoid mental landscapes and projecting those onto others. Since each Marauder is different and they are all chaotic, they represent a danger to any kind of consensual reality that other factions are building.<br /> <br /> So, you need the Technocracy to have a plausible foe that is constant if you play the Traditions mages. If you decide on a Technocracy game where the Union operates, you are playing a game where brave scientists keep the supernaturals down. Mage the Ascension first edition made the conflict more of a Cold War, which given the time made a lot of sense.<br /> <br /> Both the Technocracy and Traditions give a damn about Sleepers. Each group wants to protect the Sleepers and make them better, while well aware that strange experiments or happenings like reanimator or rains of frogs will disturb Sleepers and cause a backlash. So, with the Technocracy you get a foe fighting the war for minds with the Traditions, who is organized and who isn't necessarily evil or difficult to find a goal for.<br /> <br /> As for paradoxes and ideological issues, Mage is a game about exploring them. Different characters have different views and reality is what you make of it.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T11:09:58-07:00","id":1150706,"title":"","content":"The Technocracy it's like the Illuminati. Most of their members are humans and don't know what they're doing, but the &quot;inner circles&quot; do know<br /> Now, I think that even lowly Arete 1 Enlightened should already be part of a hidden enough circle to believe that the Consensus shapes reality. The Guide of the Techncoracy does say that the low level Technocracts don't believe to be doing &quot;magick&quot; (that what they do and what Traditionalists do it's different), but that isn't the same as to say that they don't know about the fluid nature of reality. The fear of Traditions returning the world to the DA and Dragons returning to the world has to come from somewhere after all, also the Principles of Demian and terms like &quot;reality deviant&quot; loose a lot of their punch if you're not &quot;in the know&quot;, at least to some extent.<br /> <br /> Think about mages being architects of reality: it's not so much a war between science and magic as a war to define what those therms mean. The winner get's to decide how the world works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-18-2017, 02:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T11:47:04-07:00","id":1150721,"title":"","content":"The Technocracy was originally written as THE MAN. A powerful conspiracy that had shaped the world into its modern scientific appearance and ruthlessly supressed those who pushed for a more open world. Over the various editions they've gotten smaller in scale and a lot more nuanced. Still winning, at least on the surface, but their struggle with the Traditions is not as one sided. So if it seems like there are contradictory ideas about what is going on, that's part of the reason.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-18T13:29:44-07:00","id":1150754,"title":"","content":"You really have to fully deconstruct your understanding of IRL reality, throw it all away, and start fresh when it comes to the metaphysics of Mage. Nothing works like you think it works. As immutable, reliable, and <i>constant</i> as you may believe things like our basic physical laws in our world, they simply are not in Mage. The Laws upon which the world of Mage are build are literally up for interpretation and severe editing.<br /> <br /> First and foremost: Reality is ruled by the Consensus of humanity. What the great majority of people believe, <i>is</i>. Flying surely isn't possible, right? Well, convince enough people about this weird idea called &quot;aerodynamics&quot; and how it works over curved surfaces, and eventually your &quot;flying machines&quot; stop failing all the time (Disbelief/Paradox) as more and more people believe. The Consensus slowly begins to accept that flying is possible, so long as it uses these &quot;basic principles of aerodynamics.&quot; &quot;Physics&quot; didn't make airplanes work; belief, and therefore the Consensus did.<br /> <br /> See, this is what Mage boils down to when you start looking at the Ascension War and the difference between the factions. It's all about messaging and leading the Masses around to get behind your ideas. This is how the Technocracy &quot;won,&quot; and this is where modern &quot;technology&quot; comes from. They got wise to the power of the Masses and started disseminating Technocratic &quot;methods/tools/science&quot; to everyone, got them believing, and so it spread. Previously, magic wasn't really shared outside of the people who could use it themselves, so it never became widespread. However, had the Order of Hermes mass produced magic electric wands and flying broomsticks, that would be what everyone believed in, but they didn't, so this is where we are now.<br /> <br /> Technology isn't &quot;powered by magic.&quot; In Mage, technology is <i>literally </i>magic. Look at it this way: very few people understand the principles upon which our modern-day smart phones operate. Electrical theory, electronic design, semiconductor properties, etc. That shit is straight magic to most of us. In the world of Mage, because we've all been led to believe that they work, they do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6384-frozenfishy","name":"Frozenfishy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T04:58:49-07:00","id":1150957,"title":"","content":"Thanks for your answers. <br /> <br /> It's probably due to me being a scientist (medical physics/imaging to be precise), but the problem for me is that in a Technocracy/Traditions conflict as described here, <i>there actually isn't any conflict of paradigms</i>.<br /> <br /> Both seem to (and once again, this comes from me reading the wiki, and browsing this forum) subscribe to the &quot;reality is a consensus&quot; doctrine, and the only real difference I can see is in the tools used to manipulate that consensus/reality, with the Traditions being esoteric, and the Technocracy being exoteric.<br /> <br /> f the Technocracy were to subscribe to &quot;reality is&quot;, and see it (reality) as science in general sees it (a complex system that can be described, or at least approximately described by codified, systematic, and (near) universal rules), then you have a conflict of interpretations and paradigms. You could even have the Technocracy seeing magic as a part of reality that is just badly understood, and should be systemized and analyzed (taking the Hermetic/Alchemic traditions of magic to their logical extreme).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T05:28:18-07:00","id":1150960,"title":"","content":"Ah but that's the thing; the Technocracy is trying to set those rules and codify what reality is by controlling the consensus. Remember the Precepts of Damian, which are the founding principals by which the Union stands for:<br /> <br /> Order, Stasis, and Consensus: Bring Stasis and Order to the Universe. Predictability brings safety. Once all is discovered and all is known, Unity will be won. <br /> <br /> Technology and Training: Convince the Masses of the benevolence of science, commerce, and politics and of the power of Rationality. Conflict and suffering will be eliminated in our Utopia.<br /> <br /> Safety and Security: Preserve the Gauntlet and the Horizon. Chaotic individuals who open gateways with impunity threaten the stability of our whole world. Uncontrolled portals also allow outside forces, such as Nephandi, access to our world. This must never happen. <br /> <br /> Knowledge and Surveillance: Define the nature of the universe. Knowledge must be absolute, or chaos will envelop all. The elemental forces of the universe must not be left to the caprices of the unknown. <br /> <br /> Progress and the Pogrom: Destroy Reality Deviants. Their recklessness threatens our security and our progress towards Unity. <br /> <br /> Enlightenment and Empowered Elite: Shepherd the Masses; protect them from themselves and others. <br /> <br /> <br /> By controlling the consensus they determine what reality is, and believe they can acheive mass ascension of humanity (Unity).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T06:35:37-07:00","id":1150976,"title":"","content":"The key to understanding the bit about the Technocracy not believing in magic despite all the &quot;evidence&quot; to contrary is to remember Clarke's Law; any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic.<br /> <br /> There is no magic, say the Technocrats. There's just scientific principles we don't fully understand yet. The Traditions are dangerous and need to be shut down because they're playing with forces that are not well understood and have a tendency to run out of control (i.e. paradox backlashes). They also oppose giving matches and gasoline to five year olds for essentially the same reason.<br /> <br /> So while it may look like that Euthanatos is spirit bonding with an aspect of Shiva through some repeated mantras... what's actually happening (according to the Technocrat) is that this lunatic is creating some sort of extra-dimensional resonance that is drawing a dangerous alien entity from another dimension into this one.<br /> <br /> The idea that they should NOT be mages though and that magick must be some force opposed to modernity though completely destroys the core fundamental truth of the setting... That Magic(k) is about CHANGING THE WORLD. <br /> <br /> True Magic is not ancient spells or lost secrets (though for some it can be). Its changing reality through the force of your Will and making it work the way you think it should. What you believe becomes real. So if belief in God or Spirits or True Names or the Power of You can change reality then belief in science has to work as well or the underlying metaphysics collapse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T06:55:34-07:00","id":1150982,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herbert_West</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1150957#post1150957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for your answers.<br /> <br /> It's probably due to me being a scientist (medical physics/imaging to be precise), but the problem for me is that in a Technocracy/Traditions conflict as described here, <i>there actually isn't any conflict of paradigms</i>.<br /> <br /> Both seem to (and once again, this comes from me reading the wiki, and browsing this forum) subscribe to the &quot;reality is a consensus&quot; doctrine, and the only real difference I can see is in the tools used to manipulate that consensus/reality, with the Traditions being esoteric, and the Technocracy being exoteric.</div> </div> </div> </div>Think of it like a conspiracy. After all, this is WoD. Lesser beings may believe their shit work, that what they do it's magic or science or milacre, but the enlightened &quot;know&quot; it's all the same, whatever that may be, that the enlightened shape the world arround them and so do the unenlightened albeit they don't know it. What sets mages against each other it's not so much their Paradigm (albeit this, too, can be a factor) as the kind of world they want to build. After all, the founders of the Technocracy were Hermetic, they totally believed in magic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-19-2017, 09:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T08:25:07-07:00","id":1151019,"title":"","content":"To follow on from Ajax's point - this flaw in the setting goes back to Sorcerer's Crusade, where the Order of Reason really got going. You have Cosians saying things like, &quot;Screw those Verbena and their magic herbs and moonlit dances! Their crap doesn't work. Our treatments are based on science and are therefore replicable and useful.&quot; At the beginning of that setting, most of the Order's praxes are themselves vulgar, while most Tradition praxes are coincidental, because people believed that wizards could throw lightning and such.<br /> <br /> Isn't the Cosian wrong? If everyone believes that magic herbs left out under a full moon actually produce a healing potion, isn't that just as replicable as today's science? And if the Cosian's treatments are vulgar, weren't they unreliable and useless to the common man?<br /> <br /> Mage tries to marry two ideas: &quot;science is simply magic everyone believes in&quot; and &quot;everyone used to believe in mystic magic so it worked, but now not so much&quot;. For those to be true, you need science to have been as disadvantaged a millennium ago as mystic magic is now - and why would anyone have started believing in science when it <i>didn't work</i>?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7356,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","user":"haldir2012","date":". 10-19-2017, 11:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T09:29:46-07:00","id":1151039,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151019#post1151019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To follow on from Ajax's point - this flaw in the setting goes back to Sorcerer's Crusade, where the Order of Reason really got going. You have Cosians saying things like, &quot;Screw those Verbena and their magic herbs and moonlit dances! Their crap doesn't work. Our treatments are based on science and are therefore replicable and useful.&quot; At the beginning of that setting, most of the Order's praxes are themselves vulgar, while most Tradition praxes are coincidental, because people believed that wizards could throw lightning and such.<br /> <br /> Isn't the Cosian wrong? If everyone believes that magic herbs left out under a full moon actually produce a healing potion, isn't that just as replicable as today's science? And if the Cosian's treatments are vulgar, weren't they unreliable and useless to the common man?<br /> <br /> Mage tries to marry two ideas: &quot;science is simply magic everyone believes in&quot; and &quot;everyone used to believe in mystic magic so it worked, but now not so much&quot;. For those to be true, you need science to have been as disadvantaged a millennium ago as mystic magic is now - and why would anyone have started believing in science when it <i>didn't work</i>?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because it did work. The Order of Reason didn't start out promoting a bunch of completely alien ideas. They, like the groups that became the Traditions, were already partially in the Consensus and they used those as a starting point. They didn't start out at a major disadvantage paradox wise. In fact, at the very beginning of the struggle, the Consensus was much more forgiving. In Sorcerer's Crusade the Scourge (Paradox) was a recent development and was more about making magic and technology unpredictable. <br /> <br /> And most Tradition magic was not replicatable, because most of it could only be done by those properly inducted into the various groups. Verbena magic isn't about leaving herbs out under the moon and letting natural forces work, it is about knowing which nights will work, which forces to invoke to make it happen, and having the leverage to make it happen. So even if the ideas of that potion were well entrenched in the Consensus, only sleepers with extensive training and/or initiation would be able to make it themselves. <br /> <br /> The big new idea of the Order of Reason was not that theirs was the one right way (most mages had that to a lesser or greater extent), it was that their way did not require induction or enlightenment. They held that anyone could do their works if only he was knowledgeable enough and they set out to spread that knowledge. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T09:46:37-07:00","id":1151043,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151019#post1151019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To follow on from Ajax's point - this flaw in the setting goes back to Sorcerer's Crusade, where the Order of Reason really got going. You have Cosians saying things like, &quot;Screw those Verbena and their magic herbs and moonlit dances! Their crap doesn't work. Our treatments are based on science and are therefore replicable and useful.&quot; At the beginning of that setting, most of the Order's praxes are themselves vulgar, while most Tradition praxes are coincidental, because people believed that wizards could throw lightning and such.<br /> <br /> Isn't the Cosian wrong? If everyone believes that magic herbs left out under a full moon actually produce a healing potion, isn't that just as replicable as today's science? And if the Cosian's treatments are vulgar, weren't they unreliable and useless to the common man?<br /> <br /> Mage tries to marry two ideas: &quot;science is simply magic everyone believes in&quot; and &quot;everyone used to believe in mystic magic so it worked, but now not so much&quot;. For those to be true, you need science to have been as disadvantaged a millennium ago as mystic magic is now - and why would anyone have started believing in science when it <i>didn't work</i>?</div> </div> </div> </div>This isn't a flaw of you get into the conspiration mindset:<br /> <br /> Why people decided to believe in science when it didn't work?, <i>because the Order of Reason convinced them science works</i>. That's what the Time Table it's for, you can prepare people to accept your stuff. Technocrats will prove their weapons work even if that means having their peronel shoting you with them until you believe they work.<br /> <br /> Why magic wasn't quite that replicable?, <i>because mages were greedy and hoarded their power.</i> When &quot;Paradigm&quot; states mages have the supernatural power to heal, you need to go to a mage, you can't go to mage academy to become one because such a thing doesn't exist outside occult societies. Remember, Hermetics can teach you sorcery, you just need to enter their incredibly exclusive secret society, study harder than in any school, and accept to be treated like worthless shit if you don't &quot;get&quot; what magic really is, and then you can heal people .<br /> <br /> Were the Cosians wrong?. You missed the point, the outer circle Cosians that believed they were &quot;doing science&quot; were wrong, being deliberately fooled by the Craftmasons. The inner circle, however, they knew the truth. And it wasn't a widely known truth, precisely because Paradox wasn't very present back then:<br /> The Masons genial idea, the idea that changed everything, was to provide the mundane with the tools to do magic, to tell them that their hard work can bring down milacres. Before Craftmasons you needed to be part of an elite to beneffit from the trully wonderous and milacurous (a holy person, chosen by the spirits, a learned scholar of the esoteric, etc.), the Craftmasons taught people how to do those milacres, and discovered how the Consensus worked while they were doing so (basically, they were among the first mages to actualy fight against the Consensus, the influence of people's belief was hard to miss under those circumnstances)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-19-2017, 12:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T10:52:51-07:00","id":1151064,"title":"","content":"Because... and this bears repeating... the Technocratic Union (emphasis on Union) was willing to take the collective lumps of paradox until enough people believed in the utility and repeatability of science.<br /> <br /> The Order of Reason started out with a noble goal. The problem with the Verbena's special herbs and the Order's spells was that they were exclusively under the control of a mystical elite. Only the chosen few got the secrets of healing magic. The Order's goal with Science was to open up the masses. You don't need to be a seventh son of a seventh son with a sympathetically attuned rowen wand to throw fire at your enemies... you just need this thing the Order of Reason calls a &quot;gun&quot;<br /> <br /> This noble goal of democratizing magic is what gave the early technocrats the courage to take the hits of paradox until enough people believed in what they were doing that it became coincidental.<br /> <br /> They also took anyone willing to buy into their paradigm and tens, hundreds and then thousands of willworkers willing to take the paradox hits for the cause can effect great changes.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile the Traditions continued to horde their power and secrets and exclusivity and even when they did start to organize there was no central common paradigm to their efforts. The shamans, akashics, wizards, witches and priests of half-a-dozen faiths each promoted their own beliefs with the only true unity of purpose being Order of Reason = Bad.<br /> <br /> The Technocracy won the world over to their brand of magic by presenting something that could work for anyone and being willing to endure the pain it took to get there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T11:00:38-07:00","id":1151068,"title":"","content":"Also, there are paradigm shifts in science as new data comes in. I wouldn't agree completely with Thomas Kuhn's <i>The Structure of Scientific Revolutions</i>, but in science, if you start to accumulate a mass of evidence which doesn't answer a question, alternative theories come into play. Scientists will perform experiments and debate those theories. In the Technocracy, because Control is key, there's a lot less debate than in pure research. For the Technocracy, science serves the main purpose of controlling the masses and making their lives more predictable. The Progenitors, for example, want to make everything in an organism's life cycle a matter of biology free of any supernatural origins, so they want to know how down to the molecule a drug would interact with someone. Iteration X is into time-motion studies because it wants efficiency and replicably--so that each factory worker does the same operation day in and day out for their whole working life.<br /> <br /> The Technocracy denies funding to, discredits and kills those who disagree with its view of reality. They are the man, but they are fighting for a world without the insanity of the Marauders or Nephandi and which they think would work better on science and reason than mysticism. Given how the High Age of Magic is portrayed, it probably was like we see the Medieval Age now: a bad time to not be one of the elite.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T12:02:21-07:00","id":1151082,"title":"","content":"It sounds like the masses couldn't do mystic magic merely because they believed they couldn't - &quot;only a wizard in fancy clothes should be able to throw fire with a rowan wand, I certainly can't&quot; - and thus Arete rolls were required to throw fire in 1000 AD. The Order of Reason broke through those blinders (e.g., by firing guns via an Arete roll) and told the masses that they <i>could</i> - and it was so, with normal people able to fire guns without Arete rolls. Right?<br /> <br /> I can buy that to some degree. It assumes that the Consensus not only determines what is vulgar and coincidental, but also what is &quot;normally possible&quot; (without an Arete roll) and what requires inflicting an enlightened will on reality (with an Arete roll). While the proto-Traditions had some control of the Consensus in 1000 AD, they either were too stupid or too selfish to shift it enough to allow mystic magic to be done without an Arete roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T12:34:05-07:00","id":1151087,"title":"","content":"Roughly speaking, yes. Think of it this way, every single mage has to have a good explanation for why what they are doing works. Of course sleepers would expect there must be one as well, even if they personally don't understand it. &quot;I can't throw fireballs because I don't have a pact with the devil/have access to the knowledge of the ancients/have the right place in the mandate of heaven.&quot; What the Order of Reason did was actually make their reasons available. &quot;I can't throw fireballs because I don't know how to use a gu- oh, it's just a simple point and click interface.&quot; <br /> <br /> Note, as the original discoverers of the Consensus the Hermetic houses were actually doing the same thing, but they only had a brief few years to try before the OoR took the idea and ran with it. None of the other proto-Traditions were aware of the Consensus before then. It wasn't a matter of being stupid or selfish, they just didn't have the opportunity to change their approach.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T12:49:58-07:00","id":1151092,"title":"","content":"Agreed<br /> <br /> In DA the church started to form a weak Consensus in Europe that made some powerful Hermetic spells harder to perform. Seeing that, Tremere thought that &quot;magic was dying&quot;, and jumped to vampirism. Mages don't know everything (not that they will admit such a thing)<br /> <br /> In Cruzade it's stated that mages started experiencing the Consensus as &quot;my magic it's easier at this side of the mountain, why is that?&quot;. Of course, there's quite a jump from that discovery to &quot;peasants define reality&quot;...<br /> Mages probably related this phenomenon of magic being easier or harder to some property of the land (&quot;the gods in this side are more favorable to my workings&quot;) , and things like that. Traveling european wizards seeing normal people performing rather decent medicine and fireworks in China would speak about &quot;wonders of a magical land&quot;. It was the Craftmason views about the value of normal people and hard work that allowed them to do the breakthrough. <hr /><br /> Of course, even after hearing all of this one can't get of the mind the tought that human history should habe been at least a bit different even with mages not exploiting the Consensus. We have wonders of proto-science much before Order of Reason, weren't there wonders of a more mystical nature too?.<br /> I think that history in Mage was, indeed, a bit different...after all, even in DA you can travel to &quot;the lands of the giants&quot; by foot, and that's at the end of the mythic age.<br /> I imagine that mystical lands were more common in early ages. Mystical sorcery surely was more common in ages where it was respected and teached, and, who knows, maybe a few utopical places where everyone was able to do a few miracles, like Atlantis, existed but were erased from memory by Paradox or the Technocracy. Not everything has to be the same in a world were dragons did roamed the land.<br /> I mantain that magic was likely hoarded by mages even in the dawn of history, as they had little reason to believe that normal people held so much power over the world, and probably preffered to have all the supernatural might for themselves, but not everything in the magical past HAS to be shit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T13:17:33-07:00","id":1151101,"title":"","content":"Yeah, you have to draw a fairly fine line as to who knew about the Consensus and when. If the Craftmasons didn't know about the Consensus, then they just got <i>extraordinarily </i>lucky in that their ethos of hard work and common people was the perfect way to change the Consensus. And if they did know, which implies Hermetics knew as well since early Craftmasons were mostly disaffected Hermetics, then why didn't Hermetics take action on it? I can chalk it up to Hermetics saying &quot;only those who have done enough Hermetic stuff are cool enough to do magic, so let's keep it this way with Arete rolls required for everything&quot;, but that is definitely them being selfish.<br /> <br /> Also, as a thought experiment - how would you know in 1000 AD whether your fellow Hermetic was actually Awakened or not? If the only thing preventing Sleepers back then from doing mystic magic was that they believed they couldn't, if you took a Sleeper through all the Hermetic initiation, they'd certainly overcome that mental block. If you raise your wand and throw fire, and they do the same, how do you know which of you is actually altering reality, and which of you isn't? It blurs the line between dynamic and linear magic in an interesting way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7356,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","user":"haldir2012","date":". 10-19-2017, 04:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T16:09:29-07:00","id":1151143,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151082#post1151082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It sounds like the masses couldn't do mystic magic merely because they believed they couldn't - &quot;only a wizard in fancy clothes should be able to throw fire with a rowan wand, I certainly can't&quot; - and thus Arete rolls were required to throw fire in 1000 AD. The Order of Reason broke through those blinders (e.g., by firing guns via an Arete roll) and told the masses that they <i>could</i> - and it was so, with normal people able to fire guns without Arete rolls. Right?<br /> <br /> I can buy that to some degree. It assumes that the Consensus not only determines what is vulgar and coincidental, but also what is &quot;normally possible&quot; (without an Arete roll) and what requires inflicting an enlightened will on reality (with an Arete roll). While the proto-Traditions had some control of the Consensus in 1000 AD, they either were too stupid or too selfish to shift it enough to allow mystic magic to be done without an Arete roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not only is this correct, it goes further. I would maintain that, in Mage: The Ascension, everything is magic. The differences lie in how readily people believe in it.<br /> <br /> Here's one example: automobiles. Back in the day, a horseless carriage was Vulgar magic, unless you lived in a particular Reality Zone where the local commoners believed that a wizard could make a carriage move by his magic alone. Then, in the last few centuries, technologists built - by hand - contraptions that could move under their own power. These technologists were, at first, Mages using particularly advanced Instruments to perform the Effect of moving them at greater speeds. As their forays into this budding science gained traction, they started making ones that could be used by the layman - Devices, the technomancer version of the Talisman (a type of Wonder that comes with its own Arete score). Each had to be built by hand, and required a great deal of work to repair and maintain.<br /> <br /> As these Devices became more common, the idea became accepted enough that the Mages could teach non-Awakened how to build and use them. This transitioned it into the Consensus...as an Instrument for linear Sorcery. Specifically, the Path of Conveyance, a form of &quot;hedge magic&quot; used for transporting the user across long distances quickly. Then, with Gerald Ford, the car became mass produced, as the technology had become SO accepted that the ability to operate it left the realm of Sorcery, and entered the realm of Ability. Building cars started being covered by Technology, while operating one became the province of a new Skill: Drive. What once was so esoteric that only the most cutting edge of scientists could make and use them (and then, not always safely), now became so common that it's not considered &quot;magical&quot; in the sense that it requires any special understanding. Everyone agrees that cars work, and so anyone can learn to use them.<br /> <br /> <br /> There's another example that pushes even further: Language.<br /> <br /> Language is, from a certain point of view, a form of Telepathy. Using nothing but grunts, hisses, and inflections, I can plant an idea into your head. I'm using a variant of that same process right now, just substituting mouth-sounds for arcane symbols arranged in a set order. In this case, you can read my message and have it planted in your mind, while we may be thousands of miles and hours, days, or even years apart (depending on when you read this). Yet, despite how staggering a feat it is that I can share ideas from my mind to yours, in MtAs I wouldn't need Awakened magick, a special Path, or even an Ability to do so. In M20 at least, I only have to purchase a 1 point Merit to take a Language...and that's if I want a Language in addition to my native one. This amazing form of Telepathy is so ubiquitous, a character is assumed to have some ability in it <i>by default</i>.<br /> <br /> Now imagine how long it took early Mages to get that concept introduced into the proto-Consensus of early man.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-19T16:47:07-07:00","id":1151153,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151101#post1151101\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, you have to draw a fairly fine line as to who knew about the Consensus and when. If the Craftmasons didn't know about the Consensus, then they just got <i>extraordinarily </i>lucky in that their ethos of hard work and common people was the perfect way to change the Consensus. And if they did know, which implies Hermetics knew as well since early Craftmasons were mostly disaffected Hermetics, then why didn't Hermetics take action on it? I can chalk it up to Hermetics saying &quot;only those who have done enough Hermetic stuff are cool enough to do magic, so let's keep it this way with Arete rolls required for everything&quot;, but that is definitely them being selfish.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's less that the Hermetics were selfish, and more that they were more cautious. Hermetic elitism is not motivated merely by a desire to hoard their power and deny it to their enemies, but by their desire for magic not to fall into the wrong hands. While this does include a given wizard's enemies, it also includes those who would bend magic towards dangerous ends. You, yourself, can probably think of plenty of people who definitely should not have magic, if it existed in real life. Just as people in modern times argue for regulation on the sale of guns to prevent the mentally ill or the repeat offender from getting their hands on them, so too do members of a Secret Society worry that the power of magic could fall into the hands of those who should least have it.<br /> <br /> It's for this reason that Hermetic initiation and education is so demanding. Why students must often learn all their textbooks in their original language. Why knowledge is gated behind degrees of initiation. Why the initiate is made to swear oaths of loyalty to the Order, and oaths that have them promise never to consort with demons or vampires, etc. Why the very act of performing Hermetic High Ritual Magick is so complicated, tedious, and mentally and physically demanding. The reason it is so difficult to advance in the Order of Hermes is so the Order can, organically, weed out the least desirable students. The lazy, the stupid, those prone to shortcuts, those whose reach exceeds their grasp. All of these qualities and more can lead to disaster if you give that same person access to power.<br /> <br /> Either the initiate is not stupid, or they get smart as a result of study. Either they are patient, or they learn patience. Either they are studious, or they overcome their laziness in pursuit of greater knowledge and power. If the negative qualities exist and cannot be overcome, then the initiate either reaches a plateau or washes out of the program entirely. In any case, you limit the chances of a similar thing happening to a person making bathtub semtex and bombing a mall, as happens when you spread the Technocracy's science far and wide.<br /> <br /> And even the Technocracy keeps all its most advanced tech for itself, much of it never planned to go onto the Time Table or enter the Consensus. The Union is descended from the Order of Hermes, after all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, as a thought experiment - how would you know in 1000 AD whether your fellow Hermetic was actually Awakened or not? If the only thing preventing Sleepers back then from doing mystic magic was that they believed they couldn't, if you took a Sleeper through all the Hermetic initiation, they'd certainly overcome that mental block. If you raise your wand and throw fire, and they do the same, how do you know which of you is actually altering reality, and which of you isn't? It blurs the line between dynamic and linear magic in an interesting way. </div> </div> </div>With great difficulty.<br /> <br /> Many other Traditions barely acknowledge the difference between linear and Awakened practitioners of their art. The Order of Hermes does it most often, because they are academics and love to catagorize. They would probably be the most likely to pick up on fundamental differences between an Awakened and non-Awakened Hermetic. Can the magician see the weave of magic itself? How easily can they improvise magic? What is the upper limit to this magician's abilities? Can the magician perform magic without tools, but with just her Will alone? The answers to these questions and more allow a Hermetic to determine whether one of their number is &quot;merely&quot; a Hermetic High Sorcerer, or whether she is a True Magus. And they are the only ones anal-retentive enough to subject a standardized test to everyone in their Order. You can't advance beyond a certain degree of initiation if you aren't Awakened.<br /> <br /> And, of course, since these tests are built around a Hermetic viewpoint, it's unsurprising that many medieval Hermetics saw most non-Hermetics as hedge wizards.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T03:04:55-07:00","id":1151256,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151068#post1151068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, there are paradigm shifts in science as new data comes in. I wouldn't agree completely with Thomas Kuhn's <i>The Structure of Scientific Revolutions</i>, but in science, if you start to accumulate a mass of evidence which doesn't answer a question, alternative theories come into play. Scientists will perform experiments and debate those theories.</div> </div> </div> </div>I realise the thread has moved on (in the sense that I lack the knowledge to contribute), but I'd like to address this.<br /> <br /> There is a fundamental difference between changes in the constructed model of reality (classical to quantum mechanics, for example), and a change in the idea of how to, if at all, go about constructing the model. Science remains science, the base building block of the scientific method remains utterly unchallenged (in the sense that every conceivable challenge thrown at it, it has come out on top).<br /> <br /> All too often, I see paradigm shifts thrown at science as some sort of inditement, whereas they are a cause for celebration.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T07:33:52-07:00","id":1151312,"title":"","content":"Kuhn's Paradigm theory goes past that, however. Science as you know it wasn't a thing before people like Newton, and Bacon before him, formalized the scientific method. The base building blocks of the scientific method weren't the same when Pithagoras had his mathematician cult that revered numbers, nor when Plato teached those theories. Priorities were different back then and the scale of value that allows you to measure &quot;truth&quot; wasn't the same.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T07:39:00-07:00","id":1151314,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a> - good points. Your view basically divides all actions into either dynamic magic, which requires Awakened will to perform actions insufficiently accepted by others, or linear magic, which is accepted enough to not require an Arete roll. To use your example of constructing an automobile - it's as if there's a sorcery path of Internal Combustion. Dynamic mages use their abilities to enact <i>anything</i> to make particular paths of linear magic possible, and then teach those paths.<br /> <br /> If we go back to the Hermetics in 1000 AD, they did the work to make Hermetic-style linear magic accepted and possible, but just didn't teach many people to do it. The Technocracy did the work to make, say, mass production &quot;linear magic&quot; possible, and they taught it to <i>everyone</i>.<br /> <br /> I like all of that, but it would require that certain styles of linear magic become more or less usable over time. It's not that there's a Hellfire-shaped groove worn into reality, but rather that Sleepers expect someone with a rowan wand and a pointy hat to throw fire. If Sleepers no longer expect that, it shouldn't be possible without an Arete roll to override that expectation.<br /> <br /> I'm personally fine with that - certainly the Traditions have become more focused on actual Awakened magic rather than linear magic in the last 1000 years, and this serves to explain why. It would largely remove the ability to be a sorcerer/hedge magician in the modern day, at least using mystical styles, unless you had a ton of &quot;juice&quot; to push the effect through based on latent historical beliefs, like rubbing charcoal on a pad of paper to reveal what had been written on it. So sorcerers would basically require Mana Manipulation, and Tremere would only manage to activate their Thaumaturgy through expenditure of vitae.<br /> <br /> Likewise, it should be relatively easy to be a techno-sorcerer now, and just like the Hermetics in 1000 AD, we should see plenty of Extraordinary Citizens filling the ranks of the Technocracy with relatively few Enlightened Scientists at the core. Again, this reflects the actual setting very well. I'd add that there should be a good amount of Virtual Adept sorcerers adding to that Tradition's strength.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T08:02:52-07:00","id":1151323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151312#post1151312\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Kuhn's Paradigm theory goes past that, however. Science as you know it wasn't a thing before people like Newton, and Bacon before him, formalized the scientific method. The base building blocks of the scientific method weren't the same when Pithagoras had his mathematician cult that revered numbers, nor when Plato teached those theories. Priorities were different back then and the scale of value that allows you to measure &quot;truth&quot; wasn't the same.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I dont have a high opinion of Kuhn, although he is at least not Feyerabend.<br /> <br /> Regardless, Newton to now is close to 300 years, a very significant chunk of time, during which the scientific method per se has withstood tests nigh immeasurable. <br /> <br /> What does <b>&quot;</b><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><b>the scale of value that allows you to measure &quot;truth&quot; wasn't the same.&quot;</b> mean, exactly? </span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T08:47:51-07:00","id":1151341,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Regardless, Newton to now is close to 300 years, a very significant chunk of time, during which the scientific method per se has withstood tests nigh immeasurable. </div> </div> </div>300 years it's nothing in human history. Also, in Mage, Order of Reason existed for more than 300 years: You could easily say that they fostered this Paradigm.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What does <b>&quot;</b><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><b>the scale of value that allows you to measure &quot;truth&quot; wasn't the same.&quot;</b> mean, exactly?</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div>Well, the value of experience vs models. The types of experience and models that count as valid. Individual experience vs comunal experience. Also the amount and type of proof that transforms something into objective knowledge. The importance of repeatable experiments.<br /> <br /> I lack the knowledge to make a clear and complete explanation without doing actual research, but I will try with examples: Think about Bacon's arguments against alchemists, for instance, when the man who was one of the fathers of the scientific method criticized that alchemists did all their work in their towers, never cared for practical aplications, and then obfuscated their discoveries. They rarely cooperated with others to gain knowledge.<br /> <br /> Bacon was among the first to give importance to a &quot;scientific comunity&quot; of sorts, and nowdays experiments that only &quot;work on your lab&quot; aren't treated as valid. Paradigm changed. Individual experience it's of very little value as experiments need to be repeatable and verifiable by the community. And that's just the top of the iceberg, as far as my limited knowledge allows me to see.<br /> <br /> Interestingly this implies that Technocracy isn't about doing science. They know that their tools only work for the Enlightened, and then they convince people to believe that their stuff it's nature's law and works for everyone by using social manipulation. In Mage, a lot of what humans would call &quot;science&quot; it's the result of a supernatural placebo effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-20-2017, 11:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T09:41:55-07:00","id":1151366,"title":"","content":"I have undergraduate degrees in biology and history and the Technocratic view in both is that experiments must be repeatable many times to be valid. For history, while you cannot obviously repeat an event, the father of modern history Leopold Van Ranke, emphasized archival history. If you have detailed documents kept for a purpose, these are seen as being more accurate and of higher value than oral history or the written history of ordinary people. While interpretations of historical documents differ, a good historian will base their conclusions off the evidence. However, unlike non-introspective natural scientists who see their work as not directly flowing from a test of a view of reality, historians who are introspective know that they are supplying researched and rational opinion.<br /> <br /> The very question one asks in history determines the research methods and conclusions. In biology, the same is also true, but others can apply the technical procedures while asking different questions.<br /> <br /> In Mage, magic works because someone with the power to alter reality believes, 'the universe works this way. If I use this model and these techniques, the universe will produce this outcome.' Accepted methods work because they work and if you think of Kuhn's work about paradigm shifts, a paradigm shift happens because a bunch of people make assumptions, the assumptions don't match the data and someone decides to play politics and say 'we were wrong about this, and the world operates this way.'<br /> <br /> Science is an overarching paradigm about how the world works and in Mage 20, you can see it as the basis of several paradigms: A Mechanistic Cosmos, Everything Is Data, Tech Holds All the Answers (Mage 20 main book, pages 568-571) and Turning the Keys to Reality (Mage 20 Book of Secrets, 194-195). <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T09:48:46-07:00","id":1151368,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haldir2012</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151314#post1151314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like all of that, but it would require that certain styles of linear magic become more or less usable over time. It's not that there's a Hellfire-shaped groove worn into reality, but rather that Sleepers expect someone with a rowan wand and a pointy hat to throw fire. If Sleepers no longer expect that, it shouldn't be possible without an Arete roll to override that expectation.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's both actually. If sleepers don't expect robed figures with alder wands to throw fireballs (or more precisely, they expect them not to) the consensus will be more hostile to the idea. However, for the linear magic to exist there must be an undercurrent of support for it somewhere. Not just a general acceptance, but actual support for the details. That side of the equation is usually handled by secret societies, practitioners and the like. Since the general populace usually isn't knowledgeable enough to know those details, it doesn't do much to weaken or strengthen them.<br /> <br /> That's part of the reason why sorcerers are still running around in the modern cWoD. The Consensus may be hostile to the overall idea, but there's enough support for the esoterica to leave, as you put it, Hellfire shaped grooves. The magic itself is harder and more finicky, but there are still handholds that the dedicated can follow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-20T18:16:35-07:00","id":1151520,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1150615-reconciling-the-classical-technology-as-magic-ideas-an-outsiders-question?p=1151368#post1151368\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> It's both actually. If sleepers don't expect robed figures with alder wands to throw fireballs (or more precisely, they expect them not to) the consensus will be more hostile to the idea. However, for the linear magic to exist there must be an undercurrent of support for it somewhere. Not just a general acceptance, but actual support for the details. That side of the equation is usually handled by secret societies, practitioners and the like. Since the general populace usually isn't knowledgeable enough to know those details, it doesn't do much to weaken or strengthen them.<br /> <br /> That's part of the reason why sorcerers are still running around in the modern cWoD. The Consensus may be hostile to the overall idea, but there's enough support for the esoterica to leave, as you put it, Hellfire shaped grooves. The magic itself is harder and more finicky, but there are still handholds that the dedicated can follow.</div> </div> </div> </div>And, of course, there are Mythic Threads that further help keep linear Sorcery from fading. Sure, most people don't think that wizards summoning fireballs from alder wands is real...but it's still a compelling image in their minds. A part of them still maintains those myths in their imagination. Meaning, even if literal belief in its reality is generally lacking, it still has an effect on the Consensus.<br /> <br /> Speaking of the Mythic Threads, we've seen at various times the game state that the Consensus seems to reserve spots in its metaphysical framework for beneficiaries of a sort of mythical Grandfather Clause. The Sorcery Paths work partly because they've always worked, since ancient times, and Reality is an Old Dog who loves to do tricks it already knows (if I may reference Sorcerer Revised). Same with Vampires and Werewolves, who have been around forever. While there are other reasons given in Mage's cosmology for why these two groups stick around, there's likely an element of inertia going on, too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-28T15:42:45-07:00","id":1153819,"title":"","content":"Coming in late, so some of my points may already be addressed. First, please read my article on <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/798518-the-nature-of-the-consensus\">the nature of the Consensus</a>. This is my interpretation of the Consensus, and is something of a minority view. The nutshell version is that the Consensus <i>influences</i> Reality, but doesn't <i>define</i> it. The primary motivation I had for coming up with this view was to find a metaphysics for <b>Mage</b> that doesn't make the Technocracy inherently wrong. In particular, the notion that the Technocracy is working to build on Reality's natural truths (the \u201cmaterial sciences are founded on the Earthly Foundations\u201d argument) puts some credence in their argument that there's an objective reality and that most magick just serves as a distortion away from that fundamental basis of truth.<br /> <br /> I'm also a big fan of the Technocracy being home to a disproportionate amount of Denial, the form of Quiet most closely associated with Static Synergy. In particular, that's where you get your technocrats who don't believe in magick: they're in Denial. In my games, I've taken this a step further and introduced a variation on the concept of the Marauder for those in Denial: mages who are <i>so</i> sure that what they believe is right that anything that actively conflicts with their Denial stuffers for it. As long as your Focus doesn't conflict with a Skeptic's Focus, you're OK; but do something that he considers Vulgar, and you'll find yourself being smacked by Paradox above and beyond what you'd normally face. In my world, the upper echelons of the Technocracy actively cultivate Skeptics in the Union's ranks on the theory that Technocratic Skeptics won't interfere with Technocratic operations but <i>will</i> interfere with the Union's opposition (e.g., the Traditions). That's what Conditioning is all about<b></b>: encouraging Skeptics.<br /> <br /> Basically, the distinction between the Consensus and Reality gives you a way for the Technocracy to Have A Point \u2014 that is, while they're not necessarily right about everything, neither are they entirely in the wrong. For those who see science as the search for truth and Technocrats as scientists, you have the notion that \u201ctechnomagick\u201d, at least as practiced by the Union, is an effort to ground their Focus in Reality. Conversely, the bit about Denial is there to make a place for the more conventional \u201cdark side\u201d of the Union, where it's less about the wonders of science and more about conformity. <br /> <br /> Finally, there's the matter of Enlightened Citizens. Prior to M20, this term was Technocracy speak for techno-Sorcerers. M20 redefined the term to refer to Sleepers who can use Technocratic Wonders as easily as the Awakened can. This suggests a key difference between the magick that the Traditions practice and the Enlightened Science that the Union promotes is that the latter is generally more accessible to Sleepers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 10-28-2017, 08:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}